## Testing

```java
@SpringBootTest
public class DeserializationSecurityTests {

    @Autowired
    private UserCacheService cacheService;

    @Test
    public void testJSONSerializationDoesNotAllowRCE() throws Exception {
        // Verify JSON serialization is in use
        User user = new User(1L, "testuser", "test@example.com");
        cacheService.cacheUser(user, "test-user");

        // Retrieve and verify data integrity
        User cached = cacheService.getCachedUser("test-user");
        assertEquals("testuser", cached.getUsername());
        assertEquals("test@example.com", cached.getEmail());
    }

    @Test
    public void testObjectInputFilterBlocksMaliciousClasses() throws Exception {
        // If using ObjectInputFilter, verify it blocks gadget chains
        byte[] maliciousPayload = generateYsoserialpayload(
            "CommonsCollections5",
            "touch /tmp/pwned"
        );

        assertThrows(ClassNotFoundException.class, () -> {
            legacyService.deserializeFromRedis(maliciousPayload);
        });
    }

    @Test
    public void testDependencyCheck() {
        // Verify gadget chain libraries are not on classpath
        assertThrows(ClassNotFoundException.class, () -> {
            Class.forName("org.apache.commons.collections.Transformer");
        });
    }
}
```

## Incident Response

If you suspect gadget chain exploitation:

```bash
# 1. Check for suspicious Redis access
redis> CLIENT LIST
redis> MONITOR

# 2. Check application logs for deserialization errors
tail -f /var/log/spring-app.log | grep -i ObjectInputStream

# 3. Check system for reverse shell processes
ps aux | grep -i bash
netstat -antp | grep ESTABLISHED

# 4. Immediate mitigation: Kill application and switch to JSON
# 5. Scan Redis for malicious payloads
# 6. Restore from clean backup
# 7. Upgrade all libraries
# 8. Repackage and redeploy with JSON serialization
```

